{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>When you elevate the lamps toward the face of the menorah, etc.</b> A question presses against the commentators: since 'the face of the menorah' means the central lamp, if so he should have said, 'let the six lamps give light,' to say that the six should face toward the seventh. And I say that the phrase 'toward the face of the menorah' refers to what is written above, and there is no difficulty at all. This is because we find no commandment whatsoever in this verse — for it does not say 'you shall kindle them' or 'you shall elevate them toward the face of the menorah.' Also, 'they shall give light' is not a commandment, for there is no commandment for the lamps. And furthermore, what is meant by 'Aharon did thus'? What does the verse add? And who is like him in fulfilling the word of HaShem, and what is his praise that he did not deviate? From where would deviation even arise? Rather, the Holy One, blessed be He, wished to show that He does not need its light, and for this reason HaShem wanted them all to face toward the face of the menorah, to remove from the heart of those who err. But in any case He did not wish to command about this — that he kindle them toward the face of the menorah — for none would err in this except a heedless and simple person, and would He issue a particular commandment on account of fools who corrupt? Rather, HaShem spoke by way of information, saying: I am informing you, beginning of the matter and end of the matter, namely 'when you elevate the lamps toward the face of the menorah,' for by this you will give no opportunity to those who err to err, etc.; then I too will do this — that the seven lamps shall give light forever, their light shall not be extinguished, that they shall be before Me always. And from the implication of the positive you hear the negative: if you do not elevate them toward the face of the menorah and you give opportunity to those who err to say 'for its light He needs,' then the seven lamps shall not give light at all — whether through the destruction of the Temple, or even when it stands in its building they shall not give light and the splendor of their radiance shall not shine, neither for Me nor for you." "And support for this interpretation comes from what they said in the Midrash (Tanchuma, Beha'alotkha 3), that the lamps were juxtaposed to the dedication of the altar, because Aharon was distressed that he was not included in the dedication of the altar. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: 'For something greater than this you are destined — the offerings exist only when the Temple stands, but the lamps are forever, as it says: אֶל מוּל פְּנֵי הַמְּנוֹרָה יָאִירוּ שִׁבְעַת הַנֵּרוֹת.' See in the Yalkut (Tish'it 8) for the version of this Midrash. And look to the right and see: what implies from this verse that the lamps are forever? Rather, it is certain that the author of this Midrash intended to explain the verse in this manner: if you elevate them toward the face of the menorah, then I will do that the seven lamps shall give light forever, even at the time of destruction. And the Ramban here raised a difficulty against this Midrash: behold, at the time when the offerings are suspended the lamps are also suspended; therefore he explained that it speaks of the lamps of the Hasmonean and his sons. And it seems difficult to me upon his words: behold, also the lamps of the Hasmonean were initially suspended — both alike, the offerings and the lamps — and when the land rested and was quiet, both returned to their former state; so what advantage do the lamps have over the offerings?" "And it seems to me that he took for himself a proof from the Hasmonean and his sons, that there is greater sanctity in the lamps than in the offerings — for behold, upon their restoration no miracle was performed with the offerings, but with the lamps a miracle was performed. Therefore Aharon received consolation regarding the kindling of the lamps, which is greater than the offerings. And what He said — 'the offerings exist only when the Temple stands, but the lamps are forever' — this is its meaning: the offerings, even though they had in them fire from heaven, which was an open and constant miracle, nevertheless after the First Temple was destroyed, fire never again descended, for in the Second Temple five things were lacking, among them fire from heaven (Yoma 21a). But the sanctity of the lamps never departed, even though the Temple was destroyed twice — once in the days of Nevukhadnetztzar, and once in the days of Antiochus — nevertheless a miracle was performed with the lamps, and this demonstrates the greatness of the sanctity of the lamps. And he learned this from the force of the difficulties that we have mentioned." "And it seems to me that this is the meaning of the verse: 'When you elevate the lamps toward the face of the menorah' — and you do not give opportunity to those who err to say 'for its light He needs' — then, for the greatness of the sanctity of the lamps, I will do this: that the seven lamps shall give light forever; and even at a time when there will be in them enough only to kindle one lamp, behold, by way of miracle I will do that all seven lamps shall give light in the days of the Hasmonean, and their memory shall not cease from among the children of Israel for all generations. But if you do not elevate them toward the face of the menorah, then they shall not give light forever, for I will not perform publicized miracles through them, so that they not find opportunity to reinforce their error and say: because for its light He needs, therefore He performs through them miracles more publicized than through the offerings. According to this there is in this verse no commandment at all, but rather a notification of things. And therefore it says in praise of Aharon: 'Aharon did thus, etc., as HaShem commanded Moshe' — that is to say: as though HaShem had explicitly commanded Moshe, so was he scrupulous in the matter, as though he had been commanded concerning it. And afterwards it gave the reason from where this error of theirs arose, and said: 'And this is the work of the menorah,' etc. — for all the vessels of the Tabernacle were made by human hands, except the menorah, which the Holy One, blessed be He, made Himself from a single piece: 'as the vision that HaShem showed Moshe, thus it was made' — that is to say: the Holy One, blessed be He, made it. And from here arose an opening for those who err to say: that this is why the Holy One, blessed be He, made it Himself — because for its light He needs. Therefore Aharon was zealous to elevate its lamps toward the face of the menorah, to nullify this error." "And there are those who say that what praises Aharon — that he did not deviate — is because the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him 'toward the face of the menorah shall the seven lamps give light,' and He was not particular about the elevating, that it should be toward the face of the menorah, but only about the giving of light was He particular that it should be toward the face of the menorah, and He was not particular about the elevating. And Aharon went further and performed even the elevating toward the face of the menorah, as it says: 'toward the face of the menorah he elevated its lamps.' And it is possible to explain further that the praise is that even though it says 'Aharon and his sons shall arrange it,' nevertheless he alone bestirred himself in the commandment — 'Aharon did thus' — because he saw in it extraordinary sanctity, since the Holy One, blessed be He, Himself made it, as aforementioned; and so the Ramban wrote." "And by way of allusion one may say: 'אֶל מוּל פְּנֵי הַמְּנוֹרָה' — this is the Holy One, blessed be He, who is the light of the world; 'יָאִירוּ שִׁבְעַת הַנֵּרוֹת' — for all the branches faced the central branch, and it faced toward the Shekhinah in the west; and if so, it is as though all of them faced toward the face of the pure menorah, from which, blessed be He, the light goes forth to all that exists. And from the fact that it says 'בְּהַעֲלֹתְךָ' — language of ascent — this means that he should kindle until the flame rises of itself. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said (Tanhuma BeHa'alotkha 3) that the menorah was made of itself; this is a great wonder, and in it there is an allusion to נֵר אֱלֹהִים נִשְׁמַת אָדָם — the soul of man rises upward of itself, if there is nothing preventing it on account of sins, and it is the deed of HaShem, for there are three partners in man (Niddah 31a). And we have already explained this above, in the portion of Terumah (Exodus 25:31), and the matter is sound and clear. And this is the juxtaposition of the raising up of the lamps of themselves to the making of the menorah: for it too was the deed of HaShem, as it is written: 'כֵּן עָשָׂה אֶת הַמְּנוֹרָה' — the Holy One, blessed be He, made it."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {4 "The verse citation in the second explanation refers to Numbers 8:3; the Kli Yakar quotes it without giving the address."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:40:32.547347935Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}